{"step_index":571,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-28T23:54:31Z","input_tokens":3858,"cache_read_tokens":236246,"output_tokens":1407,"thinking":"Disabling hardware acceleration and GPU features appears intended to resolve UI rendering issues, specifically black screens on Windows. Further investigation is warranted regarding the specific lines related to command-line switches used in conjunction with the initial disable call. The goal is to ensure clean UI rendering without hardware-related conflicts.\n\n\nThe combination of disabling the GPU and software rasterizer creates a scenario where Chromium has no rendering mechanism, resulting in a black screen. Specifically, the `--disable-software-rasterizer` flag, when hardware acceleration is also disabled, removes the software fallback, leading to the rendering failure. This behavior is verified through Chromium documentation and requires immediate testing to confirm the hypothesis.\n\n\nElectron's recommended approach for preventing black screens on Windows involves exclusively disabling hardware acceleration. The prior combination of disabling hardware acceleration and the software rasterizer created a critical error. Identifying the specific executables launched by the user helps to understand the application environment. Examining the C# launcher code reveals how applications are invoked, providing context for potential render issues.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"read_file","args":{"EndLine":50,"Path":"c:\\Users\\andre\\OneDrive\\Desktop\\gym\\LEGENDS\\dist\\index.html","StartLine":1}}]}
